  "slug": "self-growing-09be",
  "summary": "Recent preprints extend rare-event, linear noise, and noise-driven robustness analyses to post-transcriptional and feedback-regulated gene expression.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is how intrinsic noise in gene regulatory circuits is propagated, persisted, and statistically characterized. One item derives large deviation rate functions for post-transcriptional regulation models by mapping them onto promoter-based models via PPA, extending rare-event analysis beyond the promoter layer. A second applies a linear noise approximation to two-node transcription factor feedback motifs, showing that loop closure produces cyclic noise that sets how long fluctuations persist. The third reexamines Kaneko's evolutionary model of noise-driven robustness, reconstructing its simulation protocol and releasing reusable code; it concerns evolutionary outcomes rather than expression dynamics. What is new is the joint treatment of noise propagation, feedback topology, and rare-event statistics, though all three items are preprints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-24"
}
